<?xml version="1.0" ?>
<!--
  AUV Competition Pool World — DAVE water dynamics
  ================================================
  Migrated from camera_test.sdf. Pool geometry, props, lighting, fog AND physics
  are all unchanged from the original: the vision pipeline (YOLO gate detector,
  HSV green/blue detectors) is tuned against those exact colours and that exact
  fog, and the missions depend on the prop positions and on the AUV holding its
  spawn depth. What this world adds is DAVE's ocean-current infrastructure, plus
  a documented, commented-out path to DAVE buoyancy.

  Pool dimensions: 28 m (width, X) x 25 m (length, Y) x 5 m (depth, Z)

  Coordinate convention (world frame matching the 2D schematic):
    Origin (0, 0, 0)  = Bottom-Left corner of the schematic AT THE WATER SURFACE
    X-axis (0 to 28)  = Runs left to right (along the 28m bottom edge)
    Y-axis (0 to 25)  = Runs bottom to top
    Z-axis (0 to -5)  = Runs surface to pool floor


  WHAT CHANGED vs camera_test.sdf
  ==============================
  1. VISIBLE WATER: DAVE's animated Coast Water ocean surface at z=0, a
     water_volume tinting the pool below it, and the four walls raised 0.6 m so
     there is a dry rim above the waterline. See THE WATER section for why that
     freeboard is what makes the pool read as full.

  2. DAVE ocean current (OceanCurrentWorldPlugin + OceanCurrentPlugin), wired up
     and publishing on ROS under /hydrodynamics. The current is DEFAULTED TO
     ZERO — see the CURRENT block below for how to turn it on.

  3. Gravity stays at zero and buoyancy stays off, so the AUV holds its spawn
     depth exactly as it always has. The DAVE graded buoyancy block is present
     but COMMENTED OUT, carrying the arithmetic and the three-step checklist
     needed to switch to real weight-vs-buoyancy later.

  Mission-relevant geometry is untouched: floor at -5, every prop pose, and the
  spawn depth are all exactly as they were. Only wall HEIGHT changed, from 5.0 m
  (-5..0, flush with the surface) to 5.6 m (-5..+0.6).


  THE CAMERA FEEDS DO NOT LOOK UNDERWATER, AND CANNOT BE MADE TO FROM HERE
  =======================================================================
  Verified by rendering, not assumed. Two independent reasons:

  1. <fog> is a NO-OP in Gazebo Harmonic. See the FOG note in <scene> below.

  2. The water_volume CANNOT tint the AUV's cameras, because they sit INSIDE it.
     A hollow box only tints what is seen THROUGH one of its faces. With the
     camera inside, the near face is behind it and the far faces are behind the
     props, so the props are drawn in front of the water, not through it. This
     is geometry, not a tuning problem — no transparency value fixes it. The
     volume works for the external/GUI view and only for that.

  So making the feeds look underwater needs per-pixel work on the image:
  DAVE's UnderwaterCamera plugin (per-channel Beer-Lambert), or a ROS node doing
  the same using the depth stereo_image_proc already produces. Both are real
  work — see the FOG note for what adopting the DAVE plugin costs.


  READ THIS BEFORE EDITING THE OCEAN CURRENT PLUGIN
  =================================================
  OceanCurrentWorldPlugin does NOT null-check two of its SDF children. In
  LoadGlobalCurrentConfig() and LoadStratifiedCurrentDatabase() it logs an error
  when <constant_current> / <transient_current> are missing and then dereferences
  the null ElementPtr anyway. Deleting either block SEGFAULTS Gazebo at load.
  Both must stay present even when unused.

  The plugin also calls get_package_share_directory("dave_worlds"), so dave_ws
  must be sourced (sim_mission.launch.py handles this).


  Usage
  =====
    source ~/dave_ws/install/setup.bash
    source ~/auv_ws/install/setup.bash
    ros2 launch auv_bringup sim_mission.launch.py

  To fall back to the original world (identical physics, no DAVE plugins):
    ros2 launch auv_bringup sim_mission.launch.py world:=$HOME/auv_ws/camera_test.sdf
-->
<sdf version="1.9">
  <world name="auv_pool_world">

    <!-- ============================================================
         PHYSICS & PLUGINS
    ============================================================ -->
    <!-- Zero gravity — neutral buoyancy by suspension, same as camera_test.sdf.
         The AUV holds its spawn depth with no force acting on it. If the
         buoyancy plugin below is ever enabled, this must become 0 0 -9.81 and
         <gravity>0</gravity> must come off base_link in auv_box/model.sdf;
         weight and buoyancy only cancel if BOTH are switched on together. -->
    <gravity>0 0 0</gravity>
    <physics name="1ms" type="ignored">
      <max_step_size>0.001</max_step_size>
      <real_time_factor>1.0</real_time_factor>
    </physics>

    <plugin filename="gz-sim-physics-system" name="gz::sim::systems::Physics"/>
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-scene-broadcaster-system" name="gz::sim::systems::SceneBroadcaster"/>
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-user-commands-system" name="gz::sim::systems::UserCommands"/>
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-sensors-system" name="gz::sim::systems::Sensors">
      <render_engine>ogre2</render_engine>
    </plugin>

    <!-- ── DAVE graded buoyancy — DISABLED ─────────────────────────────────
         Off for now: the AUV holds depth via zero gravity instead, exactly as
         it did in camera_test.sdf. Kept here, with its arithmetic, because
         turning it on later is otherwise a pile of rediscovery.

         TO ENABLE, three things must change together:
           1. Uncomment this plugin.
           2. Set <gravity>0 0 -9.81</gravity> above.
           3. Remove <gravity>0</gravity> from base_link in auv_box/model.sdf,
              and add back the buoyancy_link described in step 4.
         Doing only some of these makes the AUV either sink or fly.

           4. Buoyancy needs its OWN link on the AUV — it cannot share
              base_link. Gazebo derives displaced volume from collision
              geometry, and base_link_collision is the STL bounding box
              (0.9206 x 0.8260 x 0.5814 = 0.4421 m^3). That box is right for
              contact against walls and gate posts but wrong as displacement:
              at 1025 kg/m^3 it is 453 kg of lift on an 11.7 kg vehicle, about
              38 g straight up. An open-frame AUV displaces far less than its
              bounding box because water flows through the frame. For neutral:
                  V = mass / density = 11.7364 / 1025 = 0.011450146 m^3
                  cube side = V^(1/3)                 = 0.225391 m
                  weight = buoyancy = 115.1341 N
              Put that cube on a near-massless link with collide_bitmask 0x00
              so it displaces water without ever generating contacts, offset
              ~0.05 m above the centre of mass for a passive righting moment.

         <enable> is a WHITELIST: when present, ONLY the listed entities get
         buoyancy — which is how the dedicated link gets it and base_link's
         contact box does not. A dynamic object added later (dropped marker,
         floating buoy) will not float until it is listed here too. -->
    <!--
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-buoyancy-system" name="gz::sim::systems::Buoyancy">
      <graded_buoyancy>
        <default_density>1025</default_density>
        <density_change>
          <above_depth>0</above_depth>
          <density>1</density>
        </density_change>
      </graded_buoyancy>
      <enable>auv_box::buoyancy_link</enable>
    </plugin>
    -->

    <!-- ── DAVE ocean current ──────────────────────────────────────────────
         CURRENT IS OFF BY DEFAULT (mean velocity 0 m/s) so this world starts
         out behaving exactly like camera_test.sdf.

         To enable a current, raise <velocity><mean> below. A competition pool
         has no real current, but a small one (0.05-0.15 m/s) is a cheap way to
         test whether the gate/green navigators hold station under disturbance.

         NOTE: the current will not physically push the AUV until the
         VelocityControl plugin is removed from auv_box (see model.sdf). Until
         then the plugin's value is that it PUBLISHES the current on ROS, so
         nodes can read and compensate for it.

         Gauss-Markov constraints enforced by the plugin — violating any of
         these logs gzerr at load:  min < max,  min <= mean <= max,
         0 <= mu < 1,  0 <= noiseAmp < 1. -->
    <plugin filename="OceanCurrentWorldPlugin" name="dave_gz_world_plugins::OceanCurrentWorldPlugin">
      <namespace>hydrodynamics</namespace>

      <constant_current>
        <!-- MANDATORY BLOCK — see header comment. Do not delete. -->
        <use_constant_current>true</use_constant_current>
        <topic>ocean_current</topic>

        <velocity>
          <mean>0.0</mean>
          <min>0.0</min>
          <max>1.0</max>
          <mu>0.0</mu>
          <noiseAmp>0.0</noiseAmp>
        </velocity>

        <horizontal_angle>
          <mean>0.0</mean>
          <min>-3.141592653589793238</min>
          <max>3.141592653589793238</max>
          <mu>0.0</mu>
          <noiseAmp>0.0</noiseAmp>
        </horizontal_angle>

        <vertical_angle>
          <mean>0.0</mean>
          <min>-3.141592653589793238</min>
          <max>3.141592653589793238</max>
          <mu>0.0</mu>
          <noiseAmp>0.0</noiseAmp>
        </vertical_angle>
      </constant_current>

      <!-- MANDATORY BLOCK — see header comment. Do not delete.
           Unused here: a 5 m pool has no depth-stratified current. The named
           CSV ships with dave_worlds and is only read, never applied, while
           the transient current stays unrequested. -->
      <transient_current>
        <topic_stratified>stratified_current_velocity</topic_stratified>
        <databasefileName>transientOceanCurrentDatabase.csv</databasefileName>
      </transient_current>

      <!-- <tidal_oscillation> deliberately omitted — tides are meaningless in a
           25 m pool, and the block would require a <mean_direction> child. It
           is the one optional child of this plugin that IS properly guarded. -->
    </plugin>

    <!-- Bridges the current onto ROS topics under /hydrodynamics. -->
    <plugin filename="OceanCurrentPlugin" name="dave_ros_gz_plugins::OceanCurrentPlugin">
      <namespace>hydrodynamics</namespace>
    </plugin>

    <!-- ============================================================
         SCENE & LIGHTING  — unchanged from camera_test.sdf.
         The HSV green/blue detectors and the YOLO gate detector are tuned
         against these exact ambient/fog values. Do not retune casually.
    ============================================================ -->
    <scene>
      <ambient>0.35 0.55 0.75 1</ambient>
      <background>0.05 0.25 0.45 1</background>
      <shadows>true</shadows>

      <!-- ── FOG IS A NO-OP IN GAZEBO HARMONIC. DO NOT TUNE IT. ──────────
           SDFormat parses <fog> because it is in the scene spec, so this block
           loads without complaint and looks like it is doing something. It is
           not. The ogre2 render engine never implements it: grepping
           libgz-rendering8-ogre2.so and libgz-sim8.so for "fog" returns zero
           matches, and a camera placed 18 m from the red drums with
           start=1.5 end=18.0 still rendered them fully saturated.

           This is worth knowing because fog is the obvious lever to reach for
           to make camera feeds look underwater — distance-based attenuation is
           exactly the right cue — and it silently does nothing.

           Kept, rather than deleted, so nobody re-adds it expecting an effect.

           WHAT ACTUALLY WORKS for the underwater look:
             1. The water_volume visual below. A flat tint, not distance-based,
                but it does apply to camera sensors and it does show up.
             2. DAVE's UnderwaterCamera plugin, which is the physically correct
                option: per-channel Beer-Lambert, out = e*in + (1-e)*background
                with e = exp(-range * attenuation). It needs the sensors
                converted to rgbd_camera and republishes on <topic>/simulated_image,
                so adopting it means rewiring the bridge and every vision node. -->
      <fog>
        <type>linear</type>
        <color>0.05 0.25 0.50 1.0</color>
        <start>1.5</start>
        <end>18.0</end>
      </fog>
    </scene>

    <light type="directional" name="sun">
      <pose>14 12.5 20 0 0.4 0</pose>
      <diffuse>0.9 0.95 1.0 1</diffuse>
      <specular>0.3 0.35 0.4 1</specular>
      <direction>-0.2 0.1 -1</direction>
      <cast_shadows>true</cast_shadows>
    </light>

    <light type="point" name="fill_light">
      <pose>14 12.5 -1 0 0 0</pose>
      <diffuse>0.3 0.5 0.7 1</diffuse>
      <specular>0.1 0.15 0.2 1</specular>
      <attenuation>
        <range>40</range>
        <constant>0.5</constant>
        <linear>0.01</linear>
        <quadratic>0.001</quadratic>
      </attenuation>
    </light>

    <!-- ============================================================
         POOL ARCHITECTURE (28m X, 25m Y)
         All static — real gravity does not move them.
    ============================================================ -->
    <model name="pool_floor">
      <static>true</static>
      <pose>14 12.5 -5.1 0 0 0</pose>
      <link name="link">
        <collision name="floor_collision">
          <geometry><box><size>28 25 0.2</size></box></geometry>
        </collision>
        <visual name="floor_visual">
          <geometry><box><size>28 25 0.2</size></box></geometry>
          <material>
            <ambient>0.12 0.18 0.25 1</ambient>
            <diffuse>0.15 0.22 0.32 1</diffuse>
          </material>
        </visual>
      </link>
    </model>

    <!-- North Wall (Top of map) -->
    <model name="pool_wall_north">
      <static>true</static>
      <pose>14 25.1 -2.2 0 0 0</pose>
      <link name="link">
        <collision name="wall_collision"><geometry><box><size>28 0.2 5.6</size></box></geometry></collision>
        <visual name="wall_visual"><geometry><box><size>28 0.2 5.6</size></box></geometry>
          <material><ambient>0.18 0.28 0.40 1</ambient><diffuse>0.20 0.32 0.46 1</diffuse></material>
        </visual>
      </link>
    </model>

    <!-- South Wall (Bottom of map) -->
    <model name="pool_wall_south">
      <static>true</static>
      <pose>14 -0.1 -2.2 0 0 0</pose>
      <link name="link">
        <collision name="wall_collision"><geometry><box><size>28 0.2 5.6</size></box></geometry></collision>
        <visual name="wall_visual"><geometry><box><size>28 0.2 5.6</size></box></geometry>
          <material><ambient>0.18 0.28 0.40 1</ambient><diffuse>0.20 0.32 0.46 1</diffuse></material>
        </visual>
      </link>
    </model>

    <!-- East Wall (Right of map) -->
    <model name="pool_wall_east">
      <static>true</static>
      <pose>28.1 12.5 -2.2 0 0 0</pose>
      <link name="link">
        <collision name="wall_collision"><geometry><box><size>0.2 25 5.6</size></box></geometry></collision>
        <visual name="wall_visual"><geometry><box><size>0.2 25 5.6</size></box></geometry>
          <material><ambient>0.18 0.28 0.40 1</ambient><diffuse>0.20 0.32 0.46 1</diffuse></material>
        </visual>
      </link>
    </model>

    <!-- West Wall (Left of map) -->
    <model name="pool_wall_west">
      <static>true</static>
      <pose>-0.1 12.5 -2.2 0 0 0</pose>
      <link name="link">
        <collision name="wall_collision"><geometry><box><size>0.2 25 5.6</size></box></geometry></collision>
        <visual name="wall_visual"><geometry><box><size>0.2 25 5.6</size></box></geometry>
          <material><ambient>0.18 0.28 0.40 1</ambient><diffuse>0.20 0.32 0.46 1</diffuse></material>
        </visual>
      </link>
    </model>

    <!-- THE WATER VISIBILITY SYSTEM
         ===========================
         The water_volume visual previously used visibility_flags=0xFFFFFFFE to
         hide it from AUV cameras (bit 0 cleared). This was intended to keep the
         cameras pixel-identical to the old camera_test.sdf frames.

         HOWEVER: visibility_flags in Gazebo Harmonic's ogre2 renderer is
         unreliable. In practice it often makes visuals completely invisible
         to ALL cameras (including the GUI), not just the ones with a matching
         mask, making the pool look empty.

         The flag has been REMOVED. The water_volume now renders for everyone:
           GUI camera  → sees blue water (correct)
           AUV cameras → also see blue water tint (acceptable)

         The AUV camera visibility_mask lines in models/auv_box/model.sdf are
         already commented out, so the cameras use the default all-bits mask.

         WANT THE CAMERAS TO SEE CLEAR WATER (no blue tint)? The only reliable
         way in Gazebo Harmonic is DAVE's UnderwaterCamera plugin, which does
         per-pixel Beer-Lambert attenuation in a post-process pass. -->

    <!-- WHY THE WALLS ARE 5.6 m TALL, NOT 5.0
         The walls originally spanned -5..0, flush with the water surface. With
         the water filling the pool exactly to the rim, EVERY visible surface is
         below the waterline, so there is no dry reference to contrast against
         and the pool just reads as being a different colour — which is why
         earlier attempts at "add water" looked like nothing had happened, no
         matter how the transparency was tuned.

         Raising the walls to -5..+0.6 leaves a 0.6 m dry lip above the water.
         That waterline is the cue that makes the pool read as full. Verified by
         rendering: same water settings, walls flush = invisible, walls raised =
         obviously a pool of water.

         The surface itself is DAVE's ocean, included below. -->

    <!-- ── DAVE OCEAN SURFACE ──────────────────────────────────────────────
         The same Coast Water model dave_ocean_waves.world uses. This replaces
         an earlier hand-rolled flat plane at z=0; keeping both would z-fight,
         since they occupy the same height.

         It is genuinely animated, not a static texture: the model carries a
         gz-sim-shader-param-system plugin running a Gerstner-wave GLSL shader
         with t bound to TIME, plus a scrolling normal map and a cube-map
         reflection. The mesh is 6 km x 6 km and 34k vertices, so the pool sits
         in open ocean stretching to the horizon.

         Already in the local Fuel cache (~/.gz/fuel), so this does NOT need
         network access at launch. If the cache is ever cleared, the first run
         will try to re-download it.

         TWO THINGS TO KNOW, both verified by rendering:

         1. IT HIDES THE POOL FROM DIRECTLY ABOVE. A 6 km opaque sheet at z=0
            covers the arena. To watch a mission, orbit the GUI camera BELOW
            the waterline — scroll in past z=0 and the pool interior is clear.
            The default GUI camera is not set here on purpose: overriding it
            requires declaring a full <gui> block with every default plugin,
            and a partial one silently drops the entity tree and play controls.

         2. IT IS INVISIBLE FROM UNDERWATER. The mesh is single-sided with
            normals up, so from below there is nothing to see. It adds a great
            deal above the surface and nothing beneath it. The underwater look
            comes from the haze post-process instead (auv_vision
            underwater_view_node), not from this.

         WANT BIGGER, GEOMETRIC WAVES? The model ships with <Nwaves>0</Nwaves>,
         which disables the vertex-displacement loop entirely and leaves only
         the scrolling normal map. Its amplitude/wavenumber/omega/dir params are
         all populated for 3 wave components. To switch them on, copy the model
         out of the Fuel cache into this package and set Nwaves to 3. Note the
         waves are 0.42 m amplitude and will intersect the 0.6 m pool rim. -->
    <include>
      <uri>https://fuel.gazebosim.org/1.0/OpenRobotics/models/Coast Water</uri>
      <pose>14 12.5 0 0 0 0</pose>
    </include>

    <!-- The body of water: 0 to -5 m, inset 2 cm from every pool surface so its
         faces never coincide with the floor or walls and z-fight with them.
             X 0.02 .. 27.98   (27.96 wide, centre 14)
             Y 0.02 .. 24.98   (24.96 deep, centre 12.5)
             Z -0.02 .. -4.98  (4.96 tall,  centre -2.5)

         double_sided is what makes this read as water rather than as a blue
         box: with back faces culled the volume would vanish the moment the GUI
         camera dropped below the surface. Drawn double-sided, moving the view
         underwater tints the whole scene, which is the effect you want.

         cast_shadows off — a 28 x 25 m shadow caster would black out the pool
         floor. render_order 5 puts it after the opaque props so alpha blends
         over them correctly (surface skin is 6, drawn last of all). -->
    <model name="water_volume">
      <static>true</static>
      <pose>14 12.5 -2.5 0 0 0</pose>
      <link name="link">
        <visual name="water_body_visual">
          <geometry><box><size>27.96 24.96 4.96</size></box></geometry>
          <!-- COLOUR ALPHA MUST STAY 1.0 — opacity is set by <transparency>
               ALONE. Measured in ogre2, the two COMPOUND:
                   effective opacity = (1 - transparency) * diffuse_alpha
               An earlier version used transparency 0.78 with alpha 0.22, which
               is 0.22 * 0.22 = 5% opacity: completely invisible, which is why
               the pool looked empty. Worse, driving opacity from the alpha
               channel alone renders grey rather than blue — the colour only
               survives when alpha is 1.0 and <transparency> does the work. -->
          <material>
            <ambient>0.00 0.42 0.58 1.0</ambient>
            <diffuse>0.00 0.62 0.82 1.0</diffuse>
            <specular>0.05 0.10 0.15 1</specular>
            <double_sided>true</double_sided>
            <render_order>5</render_order>
          </material>
          <transparency>0.45</transparency>
          <cast_shadows>false</cast_shadows>
          <!-- visibility_flags REMOVED: ogre2 in Gazebo Harmonic does not
               reliably honour this flag and often renders the visual
               invisible to everyone, including the GUI camera. -->
        </visual>
      </link>
    </model>

    <!-- ============================================================
         COMPETITION PROPS — positions unchanged, missions depend on them
    ============================================================ -->

    <!-- Starting Zone (140cm square, 4m from bottom) -->
    <model name="starting_zone">
      <static>true</static>
      <pose>14 4 -4.99 0 0 0</pose> <!-- Placed slightly above floor to prevent z-fighting -->
      <link name="link">
        <visual name="visual">
          <geometry><box><size>1.4 1.4 0.02</size></box></geometry>
          <material><ambient>0.8 0.8 0.8 1</ambient><diffuse>0.8 0.8 0.8 1</diffuse></material>
        </visual>
      </link>
    </model>

    <!-- Gate (Shifted right by 5m, located exactly on the 12m Y line) -->
    <model name="gate">
      <static>true</static>

      <!-- Left Post (Black) -->
      <link name="left_post">
        <pose>17.5 12 -4.238 0 0 0</pose>
        <collision name="collision"><geometry><cylinder><radius>0.05</radius><length>1.524</length></cylinder></geometry></collision>
        <visual name="visual">
          <geometry><cylinder><radius>0.05</radius><length>1.524</length></cylinder></geometry>
          <material><ambient>0 0 0 1</ambient><diffuse>0 0 0 1</diffuse></material>
        </visual>
      </link>

      <!-- Right Post (Black) -->
      <link name="right_post">
        <pose>20.5 12 -4.238 0 0 0</pose>
        <collision name="collision"><geometry><cylinder><radius>0.05</radius><length>1.524</length></cylinder></geometry></collision>
        <visual name="visual">
          <geometry><cylinder><radius>0.05</radius><length>1.524</length></cylinder></geometry>
          <material><ambient>0 0 0 1</ambient><diffuse>0 0 0 1</diffuse></material>
        </visual>
      </link>

      <!-- Cross Bar (Black) -->
      <link name="cross_bar">
        <pose>19 12 -3.476 0 1.57079632679 0</pose>
        <collision name="collision"><geometry><cylinder><radius>0.05</radius><length>3.1</length></cylinder></geometry></collision>
        <visual name="visual">
          <geometry><cylinder><radius>0.05</radius><length>3.1</length></cylinder></geometry>
          <material><ambient>0 0 0 1</ambient><diffuse>0 0 0 1</diffuse></material>
        </visual>
      </link>
    </model>

    <!-- Orange Flare (Located in the 8m-12m zone) -->
    <model name="flare_orange">
      <static>true</static>
      <pose>8 10 -2.5 0 0 0</pose> <!-- Mid-depth hover -->
      <link name="link">
        <visual name="visual">
          <geometry><cylinder><radius>0.1</radius><length>0.6</length></cylinder></geometry>
          <material><ambient>1 0.5 0 1</ambient><diffuse>1 0.5 0 1</diffuse></material>
        </visual>
      </link>
    </model>

    <!-- Blue Flare (Main Arena) -->
    <model name="flare_blue">
      <static>true</static>
      <pose>18 15 -2.5 0 0 0</pose>
      <link name="link">
        <visual name="visual">
          <geometry><cylinder><radius>0.1</radius><length>0.6</length></cylinder></geometry>
          <material><ambient>0 0 1 1</ambient><diffuse>0 0 1 1</diffuse></material>
        </visual>
      </link>
    </model>

    <!-- Red Flare (Main Arena) -->
    <model name="flare_red">
      <static>true</static>
      <pose>19 17 -2.5 0 0 0</pose>
      <link name="link">
        <visual name="visual">
          <geometry><cylinder><radius>0.1</radius><length>0.6</length></cylinder></geometry>
          <material><ambient>1 0 0 1</ambient><diffuse>1 0 0 1</diffuse></material>
        </visual>
      </link>
    </model>

    <!-- Yellow Flare (Main Arena) -->
    <model name="flare_yellow">
      <static>true</static>
      <pose>21 19 -2.5 0 0 0</pose>
      <link name="link">
        <visual name="visual">
          <geometry><cylinder><radius>0.1</radius><length>0.6</length></cylinder></geometry>
          <material><ambient>1 1 0 1</ambient><diffuse>1 1 0 1</diffuse></material>
        </visual>
      </link>
    </model>

    <!-- Target Zone (8m x 2m Green Mat at the far end) -->
    <model name="target_zone">
      <static>true</static>
      <pose>10 22 -4.99 0 0 0</pose> <!-- X=10 to offset it leftish as in diagram -->
      <link name="link">
